WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.920
What a working engineer does with this.

2
00:00:02.200 --> 00:00:06.317
On a real team, picking an open model is a small procurement process.

3
00:00:06.597 --> 00:00:16.560
Write down the constraints first: where data may go, whether you need offline use, expected volume, and whether you plan to fine-tune or host the model for others.

4
00:00:16.840 --> 00:00:22.835
Shortlist two or three models whose licenses fit, using the publisher’s license text, not the tag.

5
00:00:23.115 --> 00:00:28.533
Record the exact repo and revision, so the model you evaluated is the one you deploy.

6
00:00:28.813 --> 00:00:36.984
Run the same eval you would run on an API model, and include a frontier API in it as a reference point so you know what you are giving up.

7
00:00:37.264 --> 00:00:45.179
Then get license sign-off, and put a reminder in the calendar to re-check the landscape, because the table above will be stale by next quarter.

8
00:00:45.459 --> 00:00:51.603
The next lesson shows you how to read the rest of a model card and run a model with the Hugging Face libraries.
